{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "tLEUZhs6qV1mrsQ1G",
			"name": "Fanaticism (The God-Emperor)",
			"reference": "B136",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Unshakeable belief that the Emperor is a god and that His will justifies anything done in His name: the psychology of Sororitas, Tempestus Scions, Commissars, Inquisitors, and zealot regiments such as Krieg's or Mordian's. It is not the baseline for a line trooper; ordinary Guardsmen in Gaunt's Ghosts and the Cain novels are superstitious and observant, but pragmatic and irreverent about the Munitorum. Distinct from mere piety (Theology skill, Sense of Duty): this is doctrinal certainty strong enough to override self-preservation.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Fanaticism (B136), flat -15, taken for \"the Emperor/the Imperial Creed\" as the group/ideal. Not leveled in Basic Set. The Guardsman template carries it disabled: enable it for zealot regiments or characters, and conscript, penal and feral troops leave it off.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -15
		},
		{
			"id": "tXxIePwVJqwv2ptH3",
			"name": "Intolerance (Xenos)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"The alien is the enemy\" as baseline Imperial doctrine — not personal bigotry but state religion. Universal across Black Library fiction; only a minority of xenos-sympathetic characters (some Rogue Traders, Ordo Xenos radicals) lack it.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140). Costed at -10, the \"very broad category\" end of the range the book allows, since \"all non-human sapient species\" is about as broad a target as Intolerance can name.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tz6lxCv8dkxJ4Nl7k",
			"name": "Intolerance (Mutants)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"The mutant is the enemy\": Imperial law treats unsanctioned mutation as heresy, and ordinary citizens are shown reporting or lynching mutants on sight (Ciaphas Cain, Gaunt's Ghosts, Necromunda material).\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140), broad-category cost as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "t1A0sEdYdh4QOKkO9",
			"name": "Intolerance (Psykers)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"The witch is the enemy\": fear of untrained or unsanctioned psykers runs through Imperial culture (mobs burning suspected witches before the Black Ships arrive is a recurring Black Library beat).\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140), broad-category cost.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tdOYF7iHBS84PJ7Z0",
			"name": "Intolerance (Heretics)",
			"reference": "B140",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Hostility toward the openly faithless or Chaos-tainted specifically, as opposed to xenos or mutants generally — a narrower target (a behaviour/allegiance, not a whole species), so cheaper under B140's guidance for a less sweeping category.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Intolerance (B140), narrow-category cost (-5).",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tyDTn5TjEFpuh61hu",
			"name": "Fearlessness",
			"reference": "B55",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The iron nerve drilled (or chemically/surgically reinforced) into Commissars, veteran Guard NCOs, and Astartes alike; Ciaphas Cain's narration repeatedly contrasts genuine Fearlessness against his own merely well-hidden terror.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Fearlessness (B55), 2/level, max 5 levels as usual; buy the level that fits the character. Each level adds +1 to Fright Checks; it is the right trait for a Commissar's resolve under fear.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 2,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 5
		},
		{
			"id": "taEgXQcmbjyWIptN1",
			"name": "Unfazeable",
			"reference": "B95",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Nothing shocks or horrifies this character: the extreme end of composure, for a veteran who has seen so much warp-touched horror that a daemon incursion barely raises an eyebrow. Rare in the fiction; most named Commissars and Inquisitors show fear and master it (Fearlessness plus Will) rather than being immune to it.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Unfazeable (B95), flat 15 points. B95 makes the character exempt from Fright Checks entirely, so no human template here takes it by default.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 15
		},
		{
			"id": "toSE6D5z1zcoPsVha",
			"name": "Duty (Astra Militarum)",
			"reference": "B133",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Guardsmen do not resign; the Munitorum owns their service until death, discharge, or reassignment. Extremely hazardous, almost always active — front-line Guard regiments are shown fighting until they are no longer regiments.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Duty (B133): almost-always-on-duty, hazardous, involuntary military service, at the top of the disadvantage's standard cost range.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"base_points": -15
		},
		{
			"id": "tRKxj2wwDECiGV9XI",
			"name": "Duty (Inquisition)",
			"reference": "B133",
			"local_notes": "Lore: An acolyte or Interrogator serves at an Inquisitor's word, and an Inquisitor answers to their Ordo and (nominally) the wider Inquisition; refusal is itself grounds for suspicion of heresy.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Duty (B133), hazardous and frequent, as above.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"base_points": -15
		},
		{
			"id": "tZ30vh2oH-DtDBU90",
			"name": "Duty (Ecclesiarchy)",
			"reference": "B133",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Battle Sister's or priest's obligation to the Ministorum's chain of command — frequent and binding, but less constantly lethal than Guard or Inquisition service on average (a Sororitas' hazard comes from her *mission*, not baseline garrison duty).\n\nDesign: Basic Set Duty (B133), one tier below the fully hazardous, always-on entries above.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Ecclesiarchy"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tbgRMa4M5gz2Ejlxg",
			"name": "Sense of Duty (Squadmates)",
			"reference": "B153",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Small combat units live and die by cohesion; abandoning a wounded comrade is the one thing that reliably gets a soldier shunned by their own side, per how Guard squads and Sororitas/Militarum Tempestus kill-teams are consistently written in Black Library fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Sense of Duty (B153), small-group scope, -5.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tbUZXCRa_PCpoEA5F",
			"name": "Overconfidence",
			"reference": "B148",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A common trait of the Imperium's acknowledged elites (Scions, rising Inquisitorial stars), who are written as knowing exactly how good they are relative to line troops.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B148), -5.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tO0JLoLpaQIYnpirz",
			"name": "Callous",
			"reference": "B125",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Willingness to spend subordinates', bystanders', or entire populations' lives against the mission — a recurring trait of Commissars and Inquisitors alike in Black Library fiction (played straight in most Inquisitor protagonists, deliberately subverted by Ciaphas Cain).\n\nDesign: Basic Set Callous (B125), -5.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tnB1j-lQIOSn7JT-i",
			"name": "Legal Enforcement Powers (Adeptus Arbites)",
			"reference": "B65",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Full Arbites police powers: arrest, search, and use of lethal force under the Lex Imperialis, backed by a precinct-house and Arbites armoury. Depicted throughout Black Library (Necromunda's Arbites, the Eisenhorn books' encounters with precinct Arbitrators) as \"the law\" on any Imperial world they're posted to.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Legal Enforcement Powers (B65), 5/level; costed at level 2 (10 points) for a working Arbitrator with real jurisdiction, below the maximum the trait allows.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2
		},
		{
			"id": "tHkW-qSc94Bvq5TWQ",
			"name": "Legal Enforcement Powers (Inquisitorial Rosette)",
			"reference": "B65",
			"local_notes": "Lore: \"I have the authority of the Emperor Himself\" — an Inquisitor can commandeer Imperial Guard regiments, override a planetary governor's orders, or execute an Astartes Chapter Master's aide without trial, per repeated scenes across Eisenhorn, Ravenor, and countless codex vignettes.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Legal Enforcement Powers (B65) at its maximum, the 15-point level: the right to arrest, search, requisition and execute. LEP covers what an Inquisitor may do to people; their authority over other Imperial institutions (commandeering a regiment, overruling a governor) is Inquisitorial Rank, and their standing in Imperial society is Status. Take all three.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 3
		},
		{
			"id": "tOb0tDE5JTn7c8EfP",
			"name": "Inquisitorial Rank",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Levels: 1 Acolyte in formal service, 3 Interrogator, 6 Inquisitor, 7 senior/renowned Inquisitor, 8 Lord Inquisitor (Grand Master of an Ordo or Inquisitorial Representative to the High Lords sit at the same ceiling; Basic Set Rank stops at 8, B29).\n\nLore: The Inquisition answers to no Imperial body but itself. A single Inquisitor can requisition Guard regiments and Navy vessels, declare Exterminatus, and relieve a governor, as shown repeatedly in the Eisenhorn and Ravenor novels and codex background. In practice that authority is limited by what the Inquisitor can enforce and by rival Inquisitors.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29, capped at 8 levels), 5/level, with a custom +100% enhancement because the Rank reaches across every Imperial institution instead of one. Inquisitor at level 6: 30 x 2 = 60 points. Kept separate from Status per GURPS convention: Rank is authority within (here, over) organisations; Status is social standing.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mL1zxWhinvLE0S2-1",
					"name": "Overrides all Imperial hierarchies",
					"local_notes": "Every Imperial organisation (Astra Militarum, Administratum, Ecclesiarchy, Navy, planetary governments) treats this as a Rank higher than any of its own at the same or lower level. The Adeptus Astartes and Mechanicus are sovereign allies rather than subordinates: they are not bound by it, but in practice rarely defy it openly.\n",
					"cost_adj": "+100%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6,
			"max_levels": 8
		},
		{
			"id": "t8nz06xUx-J5kL46Y",
			"name": "Status (Inquisitor)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Nobles and planetary rulers defer to an Inquisitor who reveals themselves; most Imperial citizens will never meet anyone higher. Many Inquisitors hide their Status behind a cover identity until it is needed.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) level 5 (25 points): just below a planetary governor (Status 6) socially, while Inquisitorial Rank lets them overrule one. Interrogators typically have Status 2-3.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"base_points": 25
		},
		{
			"id": "t6P3Vir43febX6U1a",
			"name": "Secret (Works for the Inquisition)",
			"reference": "B152",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Inquisitorial agents routinely operate under cover identities; being identified as Inquisition staff can get an agent killed by exactly the people they are investigating, or by a hostile Imperial official who resents being investigated at all.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Secret (B152), moderate exposure risk, -10.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Inquisition"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "te06eH1Xf4yiU_uO2",
			"name": "Legal Enforcement Powers (Commissariat)",
			"reference": "B65",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Commissar's authority to enforce discipline within their attached regiment up to and including summary execution for cowardice, desertion, or heresy — a narrower grant than an Inquisitorial rosette (it stops at the regiment's edge) but exercised far more routinely; Ciaphas Cain's narration treats a drawn bolt pistol and the words \"for the Emperor\" as a Commissar's standard response to a wavering line.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Legal Enforcement Powers (B65), 5/level, at 3 levels (15 points) — one tier below an Inquisitorial rosette's maximum, reflecting authority that is real and lethal but scoped to a single command rather than the whole Imperium.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 3
		},
		{
			"id": "t5Cr_khJmteY-iqTC",
			"name": "Military Rank",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Astra Militarum officer rank, from a newly-brevetted Lieutenant up through a Lord General commanding an army group. A Commissar's own Officio Prefectus ranks reuse this trait under a local name (see the Commissar template). Levels are a rough fit, not a lore-exact rank chart: use as many levels as the character's actual seniority needs.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29), 5/level, generic \"Military Rank\" naming per that trait's own convention.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tOKHiPp_3p3avN8Rz",
			"name": "Religious Rank",
			"reference": "B29",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Ecclesiarchy hierarchy from parish preacher to Cardinal, or Adepta Sororitas rank from Sister to Canoness/Palatine; both use the same generic Rank trait per Basic Set's own convention of one Rank trait per hierarchy a character belongs to.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rank (B29), 5/level.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Ecclesiarchy"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tEqWsJ6wuM4c0tbmx",
			"name": "Status (Underhive Scum)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Hab-block ganger, underhive scavenger, or unregistered menial — below even the baseline hive worker in the Imperium's brutal social order.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level -2 (5 pts/level x -2 = -10).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -10
		},
		{
			"id": "tY98KxV0Lu3--4syZ",
			"name": "Status (Hab-Worker)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Registered menial or hive worker — the Imperium's baseline \"someone,\" barely.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level -1.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tw-7yJTwrTo71V8gQ",
			"name": "Status (Guard Officer / Senior Adept)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A commissioned officer, senior tech-adept, or comparable — enough standing to give orders and be obeyed without question by the rank and file.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level 2.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tST7iYIcbU-jVgYsx",
			"name": "Status (Planetary Noble / Cardinal)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Hive-noble house scion, planetary governor's court, or a Ministorum Cardinal — power that reaches across a world.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level 4.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 20
		},
		{
			"id": "tAWFIVdR06C3NIBPL",
			"name": "Status (Planetary Governor)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Absolute (if Administratum-supervised) ruler of an Imperial world.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level 6.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 30
		},
		{
			"id": "tZ7sjCAHygWPxEufg",
			"name": "Status (High Lord of Terra)",
			"reference": "B28",
			"local_notes": "Lore: One of the handful of officials who govern the Imperium in the Emperor's silent name from the Senatorum Imperialis — as high as unaugmented Imperial status goes without stepping into \"beyond play\" territory (a Primarch, a Living Saint).\n\nDesign: Basic Set Status (B28) fixed at level 8, a high but not absurd value befitting a human administrator rather than a demigod.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 40
		},
		{
			"id": "tKG-X5vfyKqww9y-9",
			"name": "Social Regard (Ecclesiarchy)",
			"reference": "B86",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Being visibly favoured within Ecclesiarchy circles specifically — preachers and the faithful defer, but it means nothing to a Munitorum quartermaster or an ork.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Social Regard (Respected), B86: 5 points per +1 to reactions, up to +4. Taken here at 2 levels, with the reaction_bonus feature scoped to the Ecclesiarchy and the devout.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Social",
				"Imperium",
				"Ecclesiarchy"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "reaction_bonus",
					"situation": "from the Ecclesiarchy and the devout",
					"amount": 1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tfNu7JEHAv_GcnnJ4",
			"name": "Act of Faith",
			"local_notes": "Once per session per level, in a crisis, the player declares an Act of Faith and picks ONE effect from this list: (1) reroll a failed HT roll to stay conscious or to resist a mortal-wound shock, or ignore the shock and stun penalty from one wound; (2) reroll a failed Will or Fright Check against fear or a daemonic or heretical influence; (3) her weapon does not malfunction on this shot (a would-be critical failure on the attack roll is an ordinary miss, and a jam clears). Not a substitute for a psychic power; do not stack with psyker abilities from the psyker power library.\n\nLore: A Sister of Battle's prayer answered at the worst possible moment for her enemies: wounds that don't drop her, a bolter that never jams when it matters, a miraculous shrug-off of a wound that should have killed her. Black Library (the Sisters of Battle novels, Dan Abnett's and others' Sororitas fiction) treats these as real and faith-triggered, not psychic. The Orders explicitly reject psychic power as unclean. The miracles are narrow and dramatic, not free-form luck.\n\nDesign: Built, not borrowed, per framework.md: Serendipity (B83, 15/level, once per session per level) with two Accessibility limitations (B110) that make it a distinct trait: -30% for \"only in a crisis (life or mission at stake)\" and -20% for \"effect limited to the survival and weapon-function list below\", total -50%, so 7.5 points per level (a two-level Sister pays 15). Does not touch the psyker power library: Sororitas Faith is explicitly not psychic power in the fiction, and this trait is built independently of it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Imperium",
				"Adepta Sororitas"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "m5042CxEDkrVnOsO2",
					"name": "Only in a crisis",
					"local_notes": "Accessibility limitation (B110). Usable only when her life or the mission is genuinely at stake, GM's call.",
					"cost_adj": "-30%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m5YUdniJ5DqezgKu_",
					"name": "Survival and weapon-function miracles only",
					"local_notes": "Accessibility limitation (B110). The effect must come from the list in the trait notes; nothing else counts.",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 15,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 3
		},
		{
			"id": "T9-48dXZqt2yIkmuP",
			"name": "Hive World Origin",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Born and raised inside a hive's vertical city-machine: gang territories, recycled air, and a horizon that is always a wall. Calibrated against Only War's Hive World home-world package (bonus to Fellowship/streetwise-type skills, poor grasp of open terrain) and against Necromunda's depiction throughout Black Library.\n\nDesign: A small optional container (net 0 points) rather than a full racial template: two Basic Set perks/quirks bought as a set, per framework.md's instruction to keep origin lenses lore-flavoured rather than mechanically heavy. Disabled by default in templates; the GM/player enables the one that fits.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tryu_2kbIW2xJ4BIX",
					"name": "Hive-Bred",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A lifetime spent reading crowds, gangs, and hab-block politics.\n\nDesign: Perk (B9), +1 to Streetwise.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": 1,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "skill_bonus",
							"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Streetwise"
							},
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tYjcgbUp2R3OO14yI",
					"name": "Never Seen an Open Sky",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Hivers are famously unsettled by true horizons and weather.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only, no mechanical effect.",
					"tags": [
						"Quirk",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": -1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T341aQNi0mLn6LLNn",
			"name": "Death World Origin",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Raised on a world that actively tries to kill its population — the Catachan/Krieg-adjacent archetype. Only War's Death World package (bonus to combat/survival skills, penalty to Fellowship from a lifetime of mistrust) is the calibration source.\n\nDesign: Net 0 points, two Basic Set perks/quirks. Disabled by default; GM/player enables it.",
			"tags": [
				"Imperium"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tmrNHdAsZp8p_3VkW",
					"name": "Death World Instincts",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A childhood spent not being eaten teaches attentiveness other worlds don't.\n\nDesign: Perk (B9), +1 to Survival.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"base_points": 1,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "skill_bonus",
							"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
							"name": {
								"compare": "is",
								"qualifier": "Survival"
							},
							"amount": 1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t8SDLokqL2MWKk4ap",
					"name": "Contempt for Soft Living",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Hive worlds and their comforts look like weakness from a death world's perspective.\n\nDesign: Quirk (B9), roleplaying flag only.",
